\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Modality} & \textbf{Bal.\ Acc.} & \textbf{Precision} & \textbf{FPR} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{R1-8B} & HS & \textbf{81.3} & \textbf{76.8} & \textbf{14.8} \\
 & Text & 73.1 & 54.4 & 22.2 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{OT-7B} & HS & \textbf{73.7} & \textbf{42.5} & \textbf{18.8} \\
 & Text & 65.6 & 34.6 & 27.1 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{R1-32B} & HS & 79.4 & 61.0 & \textbf{24.2} \\
 & Text & 71.1 & 51.6 & 32.6 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{QwQ-32B} & HS & 67.9 & 58.3 & 20.8 \\
 & Text & 67.1 & 52.5 & \textbf{17.8} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{HS vs.\ text probes at each model's validation-selected window ($W{=}15$ for R1-8B, $W{=}3$ for OT-7B, $W{=}10$ for R1-32B, $W{=}5$ for QwQ-32B). Metrics are trace-level averages. The 7-8B models (top) show consistent HS dominance; the 32B models (bottom) do not.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

At the 7-8B scale, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on both models. On R1-8B ($n{=}43$), HS probes Pareto-dominate: higher precision (76.8\% vs.\ 54.4\%, +22.4pp, $p{<}0.001$), higher accuracy (81.3\% vs.\ 73.1\%, +8.2pp, $p{=}0.005$), and lower FPR (14.8\% vs.\ 22.2\%, $\Delta{=}{-}7.4$pp, $p{=}0.044$). Both precision and accuracy survive Holm-Bonferroni correction (12 tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$). On OT-7B ($n{=}47$, matched $W{=}3$), HS probes show a parallel pattern: higher accuracy (73.7\% vs.\ 65.6\%, +8.2pp, $p{=}0.001$, survives correction), higher precision (42.5\% vs.\ 34.6\%, +7.9pp, $p{=}0.056$), and lower FPR (18.8\% vs.\ 27.1\%, $\Delta{=}{-}8.3$pp, $p{=}0.007$). Three of twelve tests survive Holm-Bonferroni, all from these two 7-8B models. OT-7B's lower absolute precision (42.5\% vs.\ R1-8B's 76.8\%) reflects its longer traces (${\sim}$80 reasoning steps vs.\ ${\sim}$30 for R1-8B): more pre-CP steps create more false positive opportunities, reducing precision without indicating weaker signal.\footnote{Precision comparisons exclude traces where neither probe produced a positive prediction, as precision is undefined in this case.}

At the 32B scale, neither model shows significant HS advantages. On R1-32B ($n{=}20$), HS probes show directional advantages in precision (+9.4pp, $p{=}0.050$) and accuracy (+8.4pp, $p{=}0.080$), with neither surviving correction. On QwQ-32B ($n{=}19$), differences are negligible: precision +5.7pp ($p{=}0.346$), accuracy +0.9pp ($p{=}0.828$).

The modality advantage is scale-dependent rather than model-specific. At 7-8B, HS probes significantly outperform text on both tested models, despite different architectures (Llama vs.\ Qwen) and training methods (R1 distillation vs.\ STILL). At 32B, no significant advantage emerges on either model, despite different training paradigms (R1 distillation vs.\ native reasoning). This is not a statistical power artifact: OT-7B ($n{=}47$) has more test traces than the 32B models, yet the 32B effect sizes are smaller ($\Delta$ precision: 5.7--9.4pp at 32B vs.\ 7.9--22.4pp at 7-8B). \cref{fig:model_dependency} visualizes trace-level FPR for three of the four models.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_model_dependency.pdf}
\caption{Trace-level pre-CP FPR by modality and model with bootstrap 95\% CIs. R1-8B shows a significant HS advantage ($p{=}0.044$); R1-32B and QwQ-32B differences are not significant. OT-7B (not shown) follows the R1-8B pattern ($\Delta{=}{-}8.3$pp, $p{=}0.007$).}
\label{fig:model_dependency}
\end{figure}

The practical implication is that modality choice is scale-dependent. At 7-8B, HS probes are clearly preferred. At 32B, the choice depends on whether the scenario prioritizes precision (favoring HS) or low FPR (favoring text). Per-model validation remains necessary, but scale provides a useful prior.

